"Displays a measured interval per category through a bar spanning its lower and upper endpoints.\n\nUse when: Each item has a meaningful start and end on one numeric scale. Interval widths and relative positions both need comparison.\n\nAvoid when: Bars would be mistaken for counts or for statistical confidence intervals.\n\nVisual grammar: Bar start and end encode lower and upper values. Bar length encodes the interval span rather than magnitude from zero.\n\nBuild: Validate and order interval endpoints for every category. Place each interval in its own row on a common numeric axis. Mark an optional central value separately and label the endpoint meaning.\n\nCheck: Check every lower endpoint is less than or equal to its upper endpoint. Verify the axis units describe both endpoints and their difference. Identify open-ended or censored intervals explicitly.\n\nPitfalls: A range is not automatically a confidence interval or a sample distribution. Avoid assigning identical lengths to intervals with different numeric spans.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic daily operating-temperature ranges for five machines using one row per machine.\n\nAlternatives: Dumbbell chart, Error bar chart, Box plot."
